While another card has the monitor, the display adapter's hardware cursor
is published as hidden, and as it really is again when the monitor comes
back.

A Voodoo 2 takes the monitor through graphic_hw_passthrough (voodoo2.c's
fbiInit0 VGA_PASS), and so does the 3D frontend showing swaps (embedfx.c).
The 2D adapter behind it goes on holding whatever cursor its driver set:
Windows' desktop pointer on d3dpt-vga stays enabled behind a full-screen
Glide game, because on real hardware nothing needs to turn it off -- the
pass-through cable shows the 3D card's picture and nothing of the 2D
card's. The console published it anyway, so the player drew Windows'
arrow over Moto Racer on the Voodoo 2, beside the game's own pointer
(2026-09-17, the first Windows host; it was never platform-specific).

dpy_mouse_set keeps storing what the device says and publishes it through
dpy_mouse_publish, which reports it hidden while the console is in
pass-through; graphic_hw_passthrough publishes again on every change, and a
listener registered meanwhile gets the same answer. A new trace event,
dpy_mouse_publish, is what the voodoo-guest-d3dpt check reads: a cursor set
on d3dpt-vga must be published shown before the Voodoo takes the monitor,
hidden while it has it, and shown after.

